<!-- Section 3: sec-what -->
<section id="sec-what" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a 5-12-13 Triangle?</h2>
  <p>A right triangle with whole-number sides in the ratio 5:12:13.</p>
  <p>A 5-12-13 triangle is a right triangle whose three sides measure 5, 12, and 13 units, or any set of lengths in the same proportion. The longest side, 13, is the hypotenuse and sits opposite the 90-degree angle. The sides of length 5 and 12 are the legs, and they meet at the right angle.</p>
  <p>It is called a Pythagorean triple because the three whole numbers satisfy <span set:html={rich('[[5^2 + 12^2 = 13^2]]')} />. That single check is what makes the triangle a right triangle, not a drawing or a measurement. Scale the sides by any positive factor and the ratio stays 5:12:13, so the enlarged or reduced triangle is still a right triangle with the same shape.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('five-twelve-thirteen-main.svg')} alt="Labeled 5-12-13 right triangle ABC with shorter leg a equals 5, longer leg b equals 12, hypotenuse c equals 13, and a right-angle marker" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Triangle ABC: legs a = 5 and b = 12 meet at the right angle, and the hypotenuse is c = 13.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: sec-pythagorean -->
<section id="sec-pythagorean" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Is 5-12-13 a Pythagorean Triple?</h2>
  <p>The squares of the legs add up to the square of the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[5^2 + 12^2 = 13^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[25 + 144 = 169]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[169 = 169]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The two sides agree, so the equation holds exactly. That is the Pythagorean theorem in action: when the squares of the two shorter sides add up to the square of the longest side, the angle opposite the longest side must be a right angle.</p>
  <p>The number 13 has to be the hypotenuse because it is the longest side, and the hypotenuse always sits opposite the 90-degree angle. The numbers 5 and 12 are the legs. Because 5, 12, and 13 are whole numbers, the triple is handy for homework, tests, and measurement work where exact answers matter.</p>
  <p>This check is the converse of the Pythagorean theorem: the theorem says a right triangle satisfies <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />, and the converse says any triangle whose sides satisfy the equation must contain a right angle. Most right triangles do not have all whole-number sides, which is what makes a triple special.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('five-twelve-thirteen-pythagoras.svg')} alt="Pythagorean theorem diagram for a 5-12-13 triangle showing 25 plus 144 equals 169" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">25 + 144 = 169, so the angle opposite 13 is 90 degrees.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: sec-sides -->
<section id="sec-sides" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Three Sides of a 5-12-13 Triangle</h2>
  <p>Three sides, three jobs: two legs and one hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>The shorter leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> measures 5 in the basic triangle. It sits opposite the smaller acute angle, about 22.62 degrees, and it is the side most students spot first because it is the shortest of the three.</p>
  <p>The longer leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> measures 12 in the basic triangle. It sits opposite the larger acute angle, about 67.38 degrees. The hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> measures 13, the longest side, and it sits opposite the 90-degree angle. When the triangle is scaled by <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, the labels stay in the same order: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5k]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12k]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 13k]]')} />.</p>
