<!-- Section 6: sec-ratio -->
<section id="sec-ratio" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the 5-12-13 Side Ratio</h2>
  <p>The ratio 5:12:13 never changes, no matter how big the triangle gets.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[5 : 12 : 13]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The shorter leg represents 5 proportional units, the longer leg 12, and the hypotenuse 13.</p>
  <p>Scaling multiplies every part by the same <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, so the ratio becomes <span set:html={rich('[[[5k : 12k : 13k]]]')} /> with the same shape underneath. The sets 5, 12, 13; 10, 24, 26; 15, 36, 39; 20, 48, 52; and 25, 60, 65 all describe the same triangle at different sizes, because each set is the basic ratio multiplied by 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5.</p>
  <p>That is why the ratio is the real identity of the triangle. Two triangles with the same ratio are similar: same angles, same shape, different size.</p>
  <p>To test a set of measurements, divide all three numbers by their greatest common divisor and see whether 5, 12, 13 is left. For 10, 24, 26 the divisor is 2, and dividing gives 5, 12, 13. For 15, 36, 39 the divisor is 3, and dividing gives the same triple. If the division leaves any other three numbers, the set is not a 5-12-13 triangle.</p>
