</section>

<!-- Section 7: sec-scale-factor -->
<section id="sec-scale-factor" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Scale Factor in a 5-12-13 Triangle?</h2>
  <p>k is the multiplier that sets the size of the triangle.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = 5k,\\quad b = 12k,\\quad c = 13k]]]')} /></p>
  <p>A scale factor multiplies all three original side lengths by the same positive number.</p>
  <p>With <span set:html={rich('[[k = 1]]')} /> you get the original triangle. With <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> greater than 1 the triangle grows, and with <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> between 0 and 1 it shrinks. The scale factor changes size, not shape: every angle stays the same and every side stays in proportion.</p>
  <p>The same factor must be applied to all three sides. Scaling only one or two sides breaks the ratio, breaks the Pythagorean check, and the result is no longer a 5-12-13 triangle.</p>
  <p>A scale factor can also be read as a ratio of matching sides: divide a side of the new triangle by the matching side of the original, and the answer is <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />. For example, a longer leg of 36 against the original 12 gives <span set:html={rich('[[k = 36 / 12 = 3]]')} />. That is the same <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> the calculator asks for, found from a measurement instead of chosen in advance.</p>
