<!-- Section 11: sec-area -->
<section id="sec-area" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Area of a Scaled 5-12-13 Triangle</h2>
  <p>Half the product of the two legs gives the area.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\frac{ab}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Substitute the scaled sides: <span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\frac{(5k)(12k)}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Simplify: <span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\frac{60k^2}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{A = 30k^2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The two legs are the base and the height, because they meet at the right angle. Halving their product gives the area of the triangle. The scale factor is squared, so doubling <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> makes the area four times as large. Area is measured in square units.</p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[k = 3]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[[A = 30(3^2) = 270]]]')} /> square units.</p>
  <p>The hypotenuse never enters the area formula, because the area of a right triangle comes from its two legs. As a check with <span set:html={rich('[[k = 2]]')} />, the legs are 10 and 24, so <span set:html={rich('[[A = (10 \\times 24) / 2 = 120]]')} /> square units, which matches <span set:html={rich('[[A = 30(2^2) = 120]]')} />.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('five-twelve-thirteen-area.svg')} alt="Area diagram of a scaled 5-12-13 triangle with base 5k, height 12k, and area 30k squared" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">With k = 2, the area is 120 square units.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-perimeter -->
<section id="sec-perimeter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Perimeter of a Scaled 5-12-13 Triangle</h2>
  <p>Add all three scaled sides to get the perimeter.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = a + b + c]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Substitute: <span set:html={rich('[[[P = 5k + 12k + 13k]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P = 30k}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Every side counts, so add all three. Perimeter grows in direct proportion to <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />: doubling the scale factor doubles the perimeter. Perimeter is measured in linear units, not square units.</p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[k = 3]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[[P = 30 \\times 3 = 90]]]')} /> units.</p>
  <p>As a check with <span set:html={rich('[[k = 2]]')} />, add the scaled sides directly: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 10 + 24 + 26 = 60]]')} /> units, which matches <span set:html={rich('[[P = 30 \\times 2 = 60]]')} />. Perimeter answers a different question from area: it measures the distance around the triangle, not the space inside it.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('five-twelve-thirteen-perimeter.svg')} alt="Perimeter diagram of a scaled 5-12-13 triangle with sides 5k, 12k, and 13k adding to 30k" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">With k = 2, the perimeter is 60 units.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: sec-find-k -->
<section id="sec-find-k" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find the Scale Factor From a Known Side</h2>
  <p>Divide a known side by its ratio number to find k by hand.</p>
  <p>The calculator takes <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> directly, but you can work out <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> yourself when you already know one side. Divide the known side by the matching number from the 5:12:13 ratio.</p>
  <p>If the shorter leg is known: <span set:html={rich('[[[k = \\frac{a}{5}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>If the longer leg is known: <span set:html={rich('[[[k = \\frac{b}{12}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>If the hypotenuse is known: <span set:html={rich('[[[k = \\frac{c}{13}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Example 1: with <span set:html={rich('[[a = 20]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[k = 20 / 5 = 4]]')} />. Example 2: with <span set:html={rich('[[b = 36]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[k = 36 / 12 = 3]]')} />. Example 3: with <span set:html={rich('[[c = 65]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[k = 65 / 13 = 5]]')} />. Enter the k you found into the calculator to get the other sides, the area, and the perimeter.</p>
  <p>Sometimes <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> is not a whole number. With <span set:html={rich('[[b = 30]]')} />, for instance, <span set:html={rich('[[k = 30 / 12 = 2.5]]')} />, and the other sides are <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5 \\times 2.5 = 12.5]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 13 \\times 2.5 = 32.5]]')} />. When you work out <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> from two different known sides, both answers must agree; if they do not, the measurements do not form a 5-12-13 triangle.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: sec-step-example -->
<section id="sec-step-example" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Example of Scaling a 5-12-13 Triangle</h2>
  <p>Scale factor k = 2, worked through from start to finish.</p>
  <p>Start with <span set:html={rich('[[k = 2]]')} /> and find every measurement of the scaled triangle, one formula at a time. Each step uses a formula from the sections above, and the summary table at the end collects the results.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Find the shorter leg: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5k = 5 \\times 2 = 10]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Find the longer leg: <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12k = 12 \\times 2 = 24]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Find the hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 13k = 13 \\times 2 = 26]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Find the area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 30k^2 = 30 \\times 4 = 120]]')} /> square units.</li>
    <li>Find the perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 30k = 30 \\times 2 = 60]]')} /> units.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The new triangle keeps the original 5:12:13 proportions: 10, 24, and 26 are all twice 5, 12, and 13. Check the right angle with Pythagoras: <span set:html={rich('[[10^2 + 24^2 = 100 + 576 = 676 = 26^2]]')} />.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Measurement</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Value</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Shorter leg a</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">10</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Longer leg b</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">24</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse c</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">26</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Area A</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">120 square units</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Perimeter P</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">60 units</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: sec-examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples of Different Scale Factors</h2>
  <p>Eight worked examples, from the original triangle to k = 10.</p>
  <h3>Example 1: Original Triangle, k = 1</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[k = 1]]')} />. Sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 13]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 30(1^2) = 30]]')} /> square units. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 30(1) = 30]]')} /> units. This is the original triangle, the smallest whole-number version of the triple.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: Scale Factor of 2</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[k = 2]]')} />. Sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 10]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 24]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 26]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 30(2^2) = 120]]')} /> square units. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 30(2) = 60]]')} /> units. Doubling the scale factor doubles every side and the perimeter, but the area grows by four times.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: Scale Factor of 3</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[k = 3]]')} />. Sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 15]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 36]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 39]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 30(3^2) = 270]]')} /> square units. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 30(3) = 90]]')} /> units. Tripling the sides triples the perimeter, while the area becomes nine times the original.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: Scale Factor of 4</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[k = 4]]')} />. Sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 20]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 48]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 52]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 30(4^2) = 480]]')} /> square units. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 30(4) = 120]]')} /> units. Each side is four times the original, and the area is sixteen times the original.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Scale Factor of 0.5</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[k = 0.5]]')} />. Sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 2.5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 6.5]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 30(0.5^2) = 7.5]]')} /> square units. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 30(0.5) = 15]]')} /> units. Halving the triangle halves the sides and the perimeter, while the area drops to one quarter.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Scale Factor of 1.5</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[k = 1.5]]')} />. Sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 7.5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 18]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 19.5]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 30(1.5^2) = 67.5]]')} /> square units. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 30(1.5) = 45]]')} /> units. A decimal scale factor works the same way: multiply each side by 1.5.</p>
  <h3>Example 7: Scale Factor of 5</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[k = 5]]')} />. Sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 25]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 60]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 65]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 30(5^2) = 750]]')} /> square units. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 30(5) = 150]]')} /> units. A scale factor of 5 gives a much larger triangle, useful for layout work where the basic 5, 12, 13 is too small.</p>
  <h3>Example 8: Scale Factor of 10</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[k = 10]]')} />. Sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 50]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 120]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 130]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 30(10^2) = 3000]]')} /> square units. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 30(10) = 300]]')} /> units. At this size the numbers stay exact, which is one advantage of working with a whole-number triple.</p>
  <p>Across all eight examples the pattern holds: sides and perimeter grow with <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, area grows with <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> squared, and the ratio 5:12:13 never changes.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 16: sec-right-angle -->
<section id="sec-right-angle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the 5-12-13 Triangle Always Keep a 90-Degree Angle?</h2>
  <p>Scaling cannot break the Pythagorean check.</p>
  <p>The scaled sides are <span set:html={rich('[[[5k,\\quad 12k,\\quad 13k]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Apply Pythagoras: <span set:html={rich('[[[(5k)^2 + (12k)^2 = (13k)^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Expand: <span set:html={rich('[[[25k^2 + 144k^2 = 169k^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[169k^2 = 169k^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Both sides match for every positive <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, because the <span set:html={rich('[[k^2]]')} /> factor is common to all three terms. Scaling stretches the triangle without changing its angles, so the 90-degree angle survives at any size. That is the same idea as similar triangles: same shape, different size.</p>
  <p>The angles never change with scaling. At every positive <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> the acute angles stay about 22.62 and 67.38 degrees, because the sine of the smaller angle is always <span set:html={rich('[[5 / 13]]')} /> and the sine of the larger angle is always <span set:html={rich('[[12 / 13]]')} />. Only the side lengths move, and they all move together.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('five-twelve-thirteen-angles.svg')} alt="Angle diagram of a 5-12-13 triangle showing the 90 degree angle and acute angles of about 22.62 and 67.38 degrees" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The acute angles are about 22.62 and 67.38 degrees, and they add to 90 degrees.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 17: sec-euclid -->
<section id="sec-euclid" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How Is the 5-12-13 Triple Generated Using Euclid's Formula?</h2>
  <p>Two small numbers, m = 3 and n = 2, generate the whole triple.</p>
  <p>Euclid's formula builds primitive Pythagorean triples from two positive integers <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[[a = m^2 - n^2,\\quad b = 2mn,\\quad c = m^2 + n^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[m = 3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n = 2]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[[a = 3^2 - 2^2 = 9 - 4 = 5]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = 2(3)(2) = 12]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = 3^2 + 2^2 = 9 + 4 = 13]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The result is the boxed ratio <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{5:12:13}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The formula works when <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> is greater than <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} />, the two numbers share no common factor, and one is even while the other is odd. Those conditions give a primitive triple, one that cannot be divided down to a smaller whole-number triple. Euclid's formula is an advanced extra: the calculator itself needs only <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, but the formula shows where 5, 12, and 13 come from.</p>
  <p>Other choices of <span set:html={rich('[[m]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n]]')} /> generate other triples. With <span set:html={rich('[[m = 2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n = 1]]')} /> the formula gives 3, 4, and 5. With <span set:html={rich('[[m = 4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n = 1]]')} /> it gives 15, 8, and 17, which is the 8-15-17 triple in a different order. The 5-12-13 triple is the member of this family that comes from <span set:html={rich('[[m = 3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[n = 2]]')} />.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('five-twelve-thirteen-euclid.svg')} alt="Euclid&#x27;s formula flow diagram with m equals 3 and n equals 2 producing 5, 12, and 13" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">m = 3 and n = 2 give a = 5, b = 12, and c = 13.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: sec-applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Applications of the 5-12-13 Triangle in Geometry and Measurement</h2>
  <p>A whole-number right angle is useful wherever exact corners matter.</p>
  <p>In geometry homework and exam practice, the triple gives quick right-angle checks without a protractor: if three lengths fit 5:12:13, the angle between the two shorter sides is 90 degrees. Teachers use it for Pythagorean theorem exercises, triangle similarity problems, and scale drawing work.</p>
  <p>Builders and carpenters use scaled versions to lay out square corners: mark 5 units along one edge and 12 along the other, and the diagonal must measure 13. Surveyors and technical drawing work rely on the same idea at larger scales, with 10, 24, 26 or 25, 60, 65 when the basic triangle is too small.</p>
  <p>The ratio also helps in mathematical modeling and classroom demonstrations, where exact whole numbers keep the arithmetic clean. For real construction, measurement accuracy and site conditions still matter, and this ratio does not replace professional surveying or building standards.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 19: sec-vs-triples -->
<section id="sec-vs-triples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Difference Between 5-12-13 and Other Pythagorean Triples</h2>
  <p>Every primitive triple is a right triangle, but each has its own shape.</p>
  <p>The table below compares five well-known primitive triples. All of them satisfy the Pythagorean theorem, yet their side proportions and acute angles differ, so each one creates a different triangle shape. Scaling a triple makes similar triangles, never a different triple: 10, 24, 26 is a scaled 5-12-13, while 3, 4, 5 is a different shape altogether.</p>
  <p>The 5-12-13 triangle is longer and narrower than the 3-4-5 triangle: its longer leg is more than twice its shorter leg, while 3-4-5 is closer to square. That difference shows up in the angles, with 22.62 and 67.38 degrees here against 36.87 and 53.13 degrees for 3-4-5. Learn more on the <a href="/pythagorean-triples-calculator/">Pythagorean triples calculator</a> page.</p>
  <p>Only the triples in the table are primitive, meaning their three numbers share no common divisor larger than 1. A scaled triple such as 6, 8, 10 or 10, 24, 26 is not primitive, because all three numbers divide by 2. Primitive triples are the basic members of each family, and every other member is a scaled copy of one of them.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Pythagorean triple</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Side ratio</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">3-4-5</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">3:4:5</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">5</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">5-12-13</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">5:12:13</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">13</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">8-15-17</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">8:15:17</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">17</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">7-24-25</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">7:24:25</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">25</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">9-40-41</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">9:40:41</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">41</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 20: sec-vs-special -->
<section id="sec-vs-special" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Difference Between 5-12-13, 45-45-90, and 30-60-90 Triangles</h2>
  <p>5-12-13 has its own angles, not the standard special-triangle angles.</p>
  <p>A 5-12-13 triangle is not a 45-45-90 triangle and not a 30-60-90 triangle. Its acute angles are about 22.62 and 67.38 degrees, and its legs are unequal, so it belongs to the Pythagorean triple family rather than either special-angle family.</p>
  <p>Use the <a href="/45-45-90-triangle-calculator/">45-45-90 triangle calculator</a> for isosceles right triangles and the <a href="/30-60-90-triangle-calculator/">30-60-90 triangle calculator</a> for the 30-60-90 family. The 5-12-13 page is the right tool when the sides follow the 5:12:13 ratio.</p>
  <p>To tell which family a right triangle belongs to, check its angles or its side ratio. Equal legs and 45-degree angles mean 45-45-90. Angles of 30 and 60 degrees mean 30-60-90. Sides in the ratio 5:12:13, with angles near 22.62 and 67.38 degrees, mean a 5-12-13 triangle. The three families never overlap.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Feature</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">5-12-13</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">45-45-90</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">30-60-90</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Side ratio</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">5:12:13</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1:1:√2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1:√3:2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Angles</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">22.62°, 67.38°, 90° approximately</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">45°, 45°, 90°</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">30°, 60°, 90°</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Equal legs</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">No</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Yes</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">No</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Main property</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Pythagorean triple</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Isosceles right triangle</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Special angle ratio</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">13k</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">a√2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">2a</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 21: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Using the 5-12-13 Triangle Calculator</h2>
  <p>Fourteen errors show up again and again in 5-12-13 problems.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Treating 13 as a leg instead of the hypotenuse: 13 is the longest side and sits opposite the 90-degree angle. <span set:html={rich('[[c = 13k]]')} /> is always the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>Confusing the shorter leg with the longer leg: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5k]]')} /> is the shorter leg and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12k]]')} /> is the longer leg. Keep the labels in order.</li>
    <li>Scaling only one or two sides: scaling only the legs breaks the ratio and the right angle. Apply the same <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> to every side.</li>
    <li>Confusing the scale factor with a side length: <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> is the multiplier, not a side. With <span set:html={rich('[[k = 2]]')} /> the sides are 10, 24, and 26.</li>
    <li>Using zero as a valid scale factor: <span set:html={rich('[[k = 0]]')} /> makes all three sides zero, a degenerate figure, not a triangle. <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> must be greater than zero.</li>
    <li>Entering a negative scale factor: a negative <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> gives negative side lengths, which cannot describe a triangle. Enter a positive <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> only.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to square k in the area formula: area grows with <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> squared. With <span set:html={rich('[[k = 3]]')} /> the area is <span set:html={rich('[[A = 30 \\times 9 = 270]]')} />, not 90.</li>
    <li>Confusing area and perimeter: area is measured in square units and uses <span set:html={rich('[[A = 30k^2]]')} />, while perimeter is measured in linear units and uses <span set:html={rich('[[P = 30k]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Adding only two sides when calculating perimeter: the perimeter includes all three sides, so <span set:html={rich('[[P = 5k + 12k + 13k = 30k]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Mixing measurement units: keep one unit for all three sides. If the legs are in centimeters, the area comes out in square centimeters and the perimeter in centimeters.</li>
    <li>Assuming that only 5, 12, and 13 work: any scaled set such as 10, 24, 26 or 15, 36, 39 is still a 5-12-13 triangle, because the ratio is the same.</li>
    <li>Assuming every right triangle follows the 5:12:13 ratio: most right triangles have other side lengths. The ratio applies only when the sides fit 5:12:13.</li>
    <li>Confusing the 5-12-13 and 3-4-5 triples: 3, 4, 5 and 5, 12, 13 are different triples with different angles. Check the ratio before choosing a formula.</li>
    <li>Rounding measurements unnecessarily: the triple gives exact whole numbers, so keep them exact. Round only when a scale factor such as 1.5 forces a decimal, and say so.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<!-- Section 22: sec-verify -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Verify a Scaled 5-12-13 Triangle</h2>
  <p>Four quick checks confirm a scaled 5-12-13 triangle.</p>
  <h3>Check 1: Side Ratio</h3>
  <p>The sides must fit <span set:html={rich('[[[a : b : c = 5 : 12 : 13]]]')} /></p>
  <h3>Check 2: Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>The legs must satisfy <span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <h3>Check 3: Scale Factor Consistency</h3>
  <p>Each side divided by its ratio number must give the same <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\frac{a}{5} = \\frac{b}{12} = \\frac{c}{13} = k]]]')} /></p>
  <h3>Check 4: Side Ordering</h3>
  <p>The sides must run from shortest to longest: <span set:html={rich('[[[a < b < c]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Use 10, 24, 26 as an example. The ratio is 10:24:26, which divides down to 5:12:13, and the Pythagorean check gives <span set:html={rich('[[10^2 + 24^2 = 100 + 576 = 676 = 26^2]]')} />. The scale factor check gives <span set:html={rich('[[10 / 5 = 24 / 12 = 26 / 13 = 2]]')} />. All four checks pass, so the measurements form a scaled 5-12-13 triangle.</p>
  <p>If any check fails, remeasure or recheck the arithmetic before trusting the numbers. A failed ratio check usually means a side was scaled by a different factor, and a failed Pythagorean check usually means the longest side was mistaken for a leg. Fix the side that breaks the pattern, then run all four checks again.</p>
</section>

